#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:22
msgid "Making subplots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:24
msgid ""
"When you're preparing a figure for a paper, there will often be times when "
"you'll need to put many individual plots into one large figure, and tag them "
"'abcd'. These individual plots are called subplots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:28
msgid "There are two main ways to create subplots in GMT:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:30
msgid ""
"Use :meth:`pygmt.Figure.shift_origin` to manually move each individual plot "
"to the right position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:32
msgid "Use :meth:`pygmt.Figure.subplot` to define the layout of the subplots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:34
msgid ""
"The first method is easier to use and should handle simple cases involving a "
"couple of subplots. For more advanced subplot layouts, however, we recommend "
"the use of :meth:`pygmt.Figure.subplot` which offers finer grained control, "
"and this is what the tutorial below will cover."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:41
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis, Frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:49
msgid "Let's start by initializing a :class:`pygmt.Figure` instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:53
msgid "fig = pygmt.Figure()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:63
msgid "Define subplot layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:65
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.subplot` method is used to set up the layout, size, "
"and other attributes of the figure. It divides the whole canvas into regular "
"grid areas with *n* rows and *m* columns. Each grid area can contain an "
"individual subplot. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:72
msgid ""
" with fig.subplot(\n"
"     nrows=2,\n"
"     ncols=3,\n"
"     figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\"),\n"
"     frame=Frame(axes=\"lrtb\"),\n"
"):\n"
"     ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:84
msgid ""
"will define our figure to have a 2 row and 3 column grid layout. "
"``figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\")`` defines the overall size of the figure to be "
"15 cm wide by 6 cm high. Using ``frame=Frame(axes=\"lrtb\")`` allows us to "
"customize the map frame for all subplots instead of setting them "
"individually. The figure layout will look like the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:92
msgid ""
"with fig.subplot(nrows=2, ncols=3, figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\"), "
"frame=Frame(axes=\"lrtb\")):\n"
"    for i in range(2):  # row number starting from 0\n"
"        for j in range(3):  # column number starting from 0\n"
"            index = i * 3 + j  # index number starting from 0\n"
"            with fig.set_panel(panel=index):  # sets the current panel\n"
"                fig.text(\n"
"                    position=\"MC\",\n"
"                    text=f\"index: {index}; row: {i}, col: {j}\",\n"
"                    region=[0, 1, 0, 1],\n"
"                )\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:110
msgid ""
"The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.set_panel` method activates a specified subplot, and "
"all subsequent plotting methods will take place in that subplot panel. This "
"is similar to matplotlib's ``plt.sca`` method. In order to specify a "
"subplot, you will need to provide the identifier for that subplot via the "
"``panel`` parameter. Pass in either the *index* number, or a tuple/list like "
"(*row*, *col*) to ``panel``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:120
msgid ""
"The row and column numbering starts from 0. So for a subplot layout with N "
"rows and M columns, row numbers will go from 0 to N-1, and column numbers "
"will go from 0 to M-1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:126
msgid ""
"For example, to activate the subplot on the top right corner (index: 2) at "
"*row*\\=0 and *col*\\=2, so that all subsequent plotting commands happen "
"there, you can use the following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:132
msgid ""
"with fig.set_panel(panel=(0, 2)):\n"
"    ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:140
msgid "Making your first subplot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:142
msgid ""
"Next, let's use what we learned above to make a 2 row by 2 column subplot "
"figure. We'll also pick up on some new parameters to configure our subplot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:147
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"with fig.subplot(\n"
"    nrows=2,\n"
"    ncols=2,\n"
"    figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\"),\n"
"    tag=True,\n"
"    frame=Frame(axes=\"WSne\", axis=Axis(annot=True, tick=True)),\n"
"    margins=[\"0.1c\", \"0.2c\"],\n"
"    title=\"My Subplot Heading\",\n"
"):\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X?\", panel=[0, 0])\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 20, 0, 10], projection=\"X?\", panel=[0, 1])\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 20], projection=\"X?\", panel=[1, 0])\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 20, 0, 20], projection=\"X?\", panel=[1, 1])\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:169
msgid ""
"In this example, we define a 2-row, 2-column (2x2) subplot layout using :"
"meth:`pygmt.Figure.subplot`. The overall figure dimensions is set to be 15 "
"cm wide and 6 cm high (``figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\")``). In addition, we use "
"some optional parameters to fine-tune some details of the figure creation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:174
msgid "``tag=True``: Each subplot is automatically tagged 'abcd'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:175
msgid ""
"``margins=[\"0.1c\", \"0.2c\"]``: Adjusts the space between adjacent "
"subplots. In this case, it is set as 0.1 cm in the x-direction and 0.2 cm in "
"the y-direction."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:177
msgid ""
"``title=\"My Subplot Heading\"``: Adds a title on top of the whole figure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:179
msgid ""
"Notice that each subplot was set to use a linear projection ``\"X?\"``. "
"Usually, we need to specify the width and height of the map frame, but it is "
"also possible to use a question mark ``\"?\"`` to let GMT decide "
"automatically on what is the most appropriate width/height for each "
"subplot's map frame."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:188
msgid ""
"In the above example, we used the following commands to activate the four "
"subplots explicitly one after another::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:191
msgid ""
"fig.basemap(..., panel=[0, 0])\n"
"fig.basemap(..., panel=[0, 1])\n"
"fig.basemap(..., panel=[1, 0])\n"
"fig.basemap(..., panel=[1, 1])"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:196
msgid ""
"In fact, we can just use ``fig.basemap(..., panel=True)`` without specifying "
"any subplot index number, and GMT will automatically activate the next "
"subplot panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:203
msgid ""
"All plotting methods (e.g. :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast`, :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"text`, etc) are able to use ``panel`` parameter when in subplot mode. Once a "
"panel is activated using ``panel`` or :meth:`pygmt.Figure.set_panel`, "
"subsequent plotting commands that don't set a ``panel`` will have their "
"elements added to the same panel as before."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:212
msgid "Shared x- and y-axes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:214
msgid ""
"In the example above with the four subplots, the two subplots for each row "
"have the same y-axis range, and the two subplots for each column have the "
"same x-axis range. You can use the ``sharex``/``sharey`` parameters to set a "
"common x- and/or y-axis between subplots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:221
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"with fig.subplot(\n"
"    nrows=2,\n"
"    ncols=2,\n"
"    figsize=(\"15c\", \"6c\"),  # width of 15 cm, height of 6 cm\n"
"    tag=True,\n"
"    margins=[\"0.3c\", \"0.2c\"],  # horizontal 0.3 cm and vertical 0.2 cm "
"margins\n"
"    title=\"My Subplot Heading\",\n"
"    sharex=\"b\",  # shared x-axis on the bottom side\n"
"    sharey=\"l\",  # shared y-axis on the left side\n"
"    frame=Frame(axes=\"WSrt\"),\n"
"):\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 10], projection=\"X?\", panel=True)\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 20, 0, 10], projection=\"X?\", panel=True)\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 10, 0, 20], projection=\"X?\", panel=True)\n"
"    fig.basemap(region=[0, 20, 0, 20], projection=\"X?\", panel=True)\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:245
msgid ""
"``sharex=\"b\"`` indicates that subplots in a column will share the x-axis, "
"and only the **b**\\ ottom axis is displayed. ``sharey=\"l\"`` indicates "
"that subplots within a row will share the y-axis, and only the **l**\\ eft "
"axis is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:249
msgid ""
"Of course, instead of using the ``sharex``/``sharey`` parameters, you can "
"also set a different ``frame`` for each subplot to control the axis "
"properties individually for each subplot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:256
msgid "Advanced subplot layouts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:258
msgid ""
"Nested subplots are currently not supported. If you want to create more "
"complex subplot layouts, some manual adjustments are needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:261
msgid ""
"The following example draws three subplots in a 2-row, 2-column layout, with "
"the first subplot occupying the first row."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:266
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"# Bottom row, two subplots\n"
"with fig.subplot(nrows=1, ncols=2, figsize=(\"15c\", \"3c\"), tag=\"b)\"):\n"
"    fig.basemap(\n"
"        region=[0, 5, 0, 5],\n"
"        projection=\"X?\",\n"
"        frame=Frame(axes=\"WSne\", axis=Axis(annot=True, tick=True)),\n"
"        panel=[0, 0],\n"
"    )\n"
"    fig.basemap(\n"
"        region=[0, 5, 0, 5],\n"
"        projection=\"X?\",\n"
"        frame=Frame(axes=\"WSne\", axis=Axis(annot=True, tick=True)),\n"
"        panel=[0, 1],\n"
"    )\n"
"# Move plot origin by 1 cm above the height of the entire figure\n"
"fig.shift_origin(yshift=\"h+1c\")\n"
"# Top row, one subplot\n"
"with fig.subplot(nrows=1, ncols=1, figsize=(\"15c\", \"3c\"), tag=\"a)\"):\n"
"    fig.basemap(\n"
"        region=[0, 10, 0, 10],\n"
"        projection=\"X?\",\n"
"        frame=Frame(axes=\"WSne\", axis=Axis(annot=True, tick=True)),\n"
"        panel=[0, 0],\n"
"    )\n"
"    fig.text(text=\"TEXT\", x=5, y=5)\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:301
msgid ""
"We start by drawing the bottom two subplots, setting ``tag=\"b)\"`` so that "
"the subplots are tagged 'b)' and 'c)'. Next, we use :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"shift_origin` to move the plot origin 1 cm above the **h**\\ eight of the "
"entire figure that is currently plotted (i.e. the bottom row subplots). A "
"single subplot is then plotted on the top row. You may need to adjust the "
"``yshift`` parameter to make your plot look nice. This top row uses "
"``tag=\"a)\"``, and we also plotted some text inside. Note that "
"``projection=\"X?\"`` was used to let GMT automatically determine the size "
"of the subplot according to the size of the subplot area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/subplots.rst:312
msgid ""
"You can also manually override the ``tag`` for each subplot using for "
"example, ``fig.set_panel(..., tag=\"b) Panel 2\")`` which would allow you to "
"manually tag a single subplot as you wish. This can be useful for adding a "
"more descriptive subtitle to individual subplots."
msgstr ""

